.TH SYSTEMD\-MCPD 1 "2026-08-12" "systemd-mcpd" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
systemd\-mcpd \- Model Context Protocol server exposing systemd
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B systemd\-mcpd
.BI \-\-grant " scope" [, scope ...]
.br
.B systemd\-mcpd
.RB [ \-\-help | \-\-version ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B systemd\-mcpd
serves the Model Context Protocol over stdin and stdout, exposing a
capability\-scoped view of systemd to a language\-model client: unit
state, unit properties, timers, sockets, unit files, dependency edges,
journal entries, boot timings, and, where granted, unit lifecycle and
enablement changes.
.PP
The transport is line\-delimited JSON\-RPC 2.0. The server reads
requests until end of file and exits 0. It is normally started by an
MCP client as a child process rather than run interactively.
.PP
Nothing is exposed by default. The server refuses to start unless
.B \-\-grant
names at least one scope. Tools outside the granted scopes are neither
advertised nor callable.
.PP
Data comes from PID 1's varlink socket where available (systemd 258 and
later) and from
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR journalctl (1)
and
.BR systemd\-analyze (1)
otherwise. The reply is the same either way.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-grant " scope" [, scope ...]
Grant capability scopes, comma separated. Required. An unknown scope is
an error, not a warning.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH SCOPES
.TP
.B units:read
Unit state, properties, timers, sockets, unit files, dependency edges,
and the sandboxing exposure analysis of one unit.
.TP
.B journal:read
Journal entries for one unit, and the boots recorded in the journal.
.TP
.B boot:read
Boot phase timings, the critical chain, and per\-unit startup times.
.TP
.B units:write
Unit lifecycle (start, stop, restart, reload), enablement (enable,
disable, mask, unmask) and log\-control changes. Every change is planned
first and applied by plan identifier; an apply is refused if the state
changed since the plan was made.
.PP
Scopes are independent. Granting
.B units:write
grants no read scope, and neither implies the other.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The client closed the connection.
.TP
.B 1
No scope was granted, an argument was unknown, or the streams failed.
.SH SECURITY
The server runs with the privileges of the process that started it and
acquires no others. It is not setuid and does not read the environment
for configuration. An unprivileged instance can plan any change but
apply only what
.BR polkit (8)
permits; the refusal is systemctl's, passed through unchanged.
.PP
Reading the complete system journal requires membership in the
.B systemd\-journal
group. The shipped unit file arranges this and runs the server under
.BR DynamicUser= .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/system/systemd\-mcpd.socket
Socket unit, \fBAccept=yes\fR, mode 0600. The unit to enable if you want
a supervised instance. Not required when an MCP client spawns the server
directly, which is the usual case.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/system/systemd\-mcpd@.service
Hardened template, instantiated per connection by the socket unit.
.TP
.I /run/systemd\-mcpd.sock
Where the socket unit listens. Anything that can open it gets the scopes
the service grants.
.TP
.I /run/systemd/io.systemd.Manager
PID 1's varlink socket, used where present.
.SH EXAMPLES
Read\-only, as an MCP client would start it:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
systemd\-mcpd \-\-grant units:read,journal:read,boot:read
.EE
.RE
.PP
Read and change unit state:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
systemd\-mcpd \-\-grant units:read,units:write
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR journalctl (1),
.BR systemd\-analyze (1),
.BR systemd.service (5)
.PP
Protocol and tool reference:
.UR https://github.com/rhaist/systemd-mcpd
.UE
.SH AUTHOR
Robert Haist.
